---
import ProseLayout from '@/layouts/ProseLayout.astro';

// A real ordered sequence (docs/editorial/workflow.md), not a decorative
// 01/02/03 - a piece genuinely cannot skip a stage, and the order itself is
// the information worth showing, so it earns the same step-trace visual
// language as ArticleLayout.astro's progress rail rather than a plain list.
const stages = [
  'Idea',
  'Researching',
  'Source-verified',
  'Outlined',
  'Drafting',
  'Technical review',
  'Editorial review',
];
---

<ProseLayout
  eyebrow="Editorial policy"
  title="Review and approval"
  description="How Runtime Signals reviews and approves content before it publishes."
  canonicalPath="/editorial-policy"
>
  <ol class="workflow-trace" aria-label="Editorial workflow stages, in order">
    {
      stages.map((stage, i) => (
        <li>
          <span class="workflow-trace__mark" aria-hidden="true">
            {String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
          </span>
          <span class="workflow-trace__label">{stage}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
  <p>That workflow tracks readiness; it does not grant permission to publish.</p>
  <p>
    Publication authorization is separate and never comes from the content itself. It requires a
    merged, reviewed pull request whose required checks pass, whose CODEOWNERS approval is recorded,
    and whose deployment environment is authorized - captured for the exact commit being built.
    Nothing an author writes in a draft, including a status field, can grant that authorization.
  </p>
  <h2>AI assistance</h2>
  <p>
    LLMs may assist with source triage, metadata extraction, outline suggestions, citation-gap
    detection, and draft critique. No unreviewed AI-generated article is ever published; every
    AI-assisted step is recorded - model, prompt version, inputs, outputs, and the human reviewer's
    decision - and hidden reasoning is never stored.
  </p>
  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    A correction does not erase history. It creates a visible revision note, keeps the article's
    stable URL, and states what changed and why. See <a href="/corrections">corrections</a>.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>

<style>
  .workflow-trace {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
    padding: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .workflow-trace li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4) var(--space-3) 0;
    position: relative;
  }

  /* A connecting line between stages, desktop only - on narrow viewports the
     list wraps and a horizontal connector would misalign, so the hairline
     top/bottom border above is the connector there instead. */
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .workflow-trace li:not(:last-child)::after {
      content: '';
      position: absolute;
      right: 0;
      top: 50%;
      width: var(--space-4);
      height: 1px;
      background: var(--hairline);
    }
  }

  .workflow-trace__mark {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--signal);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .workflow-trace__label {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 39.99rem) {
    .workflow-trace {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .workflow-trace li {
      min-block-size: var(--touch-target-comfortable);
      padding-block: var(--space-2);
      border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    }

    .workflow-trace li:first-child {
      border-top: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
